/*! \page developer_backend_page Supporting GPU and CPU Backends

\ref developer_container_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_test_page      (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package builds two executable files, that are pscf_rpc and 
pscf_rpg, that are designed to solve the same set of problems involving 
real periodic fields, but that are specialized to use different hardware. 
The pscf_rpg executable makes use of a GPU, while the pscf_rpg executable 
uses only a CPU.

The differences between two programs arise primarily from differences in:

   - Where are large arrays of data stored (in CPU vs. GPU memory) ?

   - Where and how are mathematical operations on such arrays performed 
     (on the CPU or GPU)?

We summarize these differences in what follows by saying that these two 
programs use different computational "backends".  In pscf_rpg, which 
uses a Cuda backend, almost all large arrays are allocated in GPU global 
memory, and basic mathematical operations that are performed on these 
arrays are generally performed on the GPU within CUDA kernels.  In 
pscf_rpc, which uses a C++ backend, all data structures that use heap 
memory are allocated in CPU main memory, and all computations are 
performed on the CPU.

The system used by PSCF to support the use of more than one backend is 
based on the use of C++ class templates to minimize code duplication. 
The subpages linked below describe this system.

